@page "/done"
@rendermode @(new InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(prerender: false))

@using BgQuiz_Blazor.Client.Quiz

@inject QuizController Controller
@inject PickedProblemFolder Folder
@inject QuizStatsStore StatsStore
@inject QuizSettings Settings
@inject MixVisibility MixVisibility
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject QuizLiveMarker Marker
@inject QuizNoticeDismissal Notices

<PageTitle>BgQuiz — Done</PageTitle>

@* app-busy: progress cursor while a Restart transition runs (app.css). *@
<div class="container py-3 @(Controller.IsBusy ? "app-busy" : null)">
    <h1>Quiz complete</h1>

    @* Active-context stats notices, mirrored from Quiz: a failure on the FINAL
       Continue lands the user here without ever seeing the in-quiz notice, so
       Done must state it too. No subscription needed — the status cannot
       change while this page is shown (no folds happen here; Restart
       navigates away and re-binds). *@
    @* The retirement report, mirrored from Quiz for the same reason the notices
       below are: what happened to the stats context is exactly what someone
       reading their results wants to know, and "your lifetime record restarted"
       is the most consequential thing that can have happened to it.

       Dismissible, like its neighbours and like every notice on every page
       (SPEC-notices.md Fork B, which overturned this page's earlier "read
       once" exception): one rule, not one per page. These are the same
       notices as the Quiz page's, so they bind to the same QuizNoticeDismissal
       slot and the same occurrence — one occurrence, one dismissal: a notice
       dismissed on the Quiz page stays dismissed here, and the next occurrence
       shows fresh on both. *@
    @if (StatsStore.StatsRetiredOccurrence is { } retired)
    {
        <Notice Kind="NoticeKind.Information" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite"
                Dismissible="true"
                @bind-Dismissed:get="Notices.IsDismissed(QuizNotice.StatsRetired, retired)"
                @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => Notices.Dismiss(QuizNotice.StatsRetired, retired)">
            Your stats file was written by an earlier version of BgQuiz and can't be
            carried forward. It has been set aside as
            <strong>@retired.SetAsideFileName</strong> and a new
            <strong>@QuizStatsFile.FileName</strong> started, so your lifetime stats
            began again with this quiz.
        </Notice>
    }

    @if (StatsStore.Status == QuizStatsStatus.LoadFailed)
    {
        <Notice Kind="NoticeKind.Warning" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite"
                Dismissible="true"
                @bind-Dismissed:get="Notices.IsDismissed(QuizNotice.StatsContext, StatsStore.StatusOccurrence)"
                @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => Notices.Dismiss(QuizNotice.StatsContext, StatsStore.StatusOccurrence)">
            The existing <strong>@QuizStatsFile.FileName</strong> couldn't be read, so this
            quiz didn't record stats. The file has not been changed.
        </Notice>
    }
    else if (StatsStore.Status == QuizStatsStatus.WriteFailed)
    {
        <Notice Kind="NoticeKind.Error" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Assertive"
                Dismissible="true"
                @bind-Dismissed:get="Notices.IsDismissed(QuizNotice.StatsContext, StatsStore.StatusOccurrence)"
                @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => Notices.Dismiss(QuizNotice.StatsContext, StatsStore.StatusOccurrence)">
            Your stats file could not be saved — this quiz's stats were not recorded.
        </Notice>
    }

    <ScorePanel Score="Controller.Score"
                Skipped="Controller.SkippedCount"
                Source="@Controller.Name"
                Heading="Final" />

    @* The problem folder leads the breakdown's heading, on its line
       (halheinrich/backgammon#199): the folder held NOW, by ruling — a re-pick
       mid-quiz does not bear on this, so no run-captured copy exists. The
       quoting is the holder's DisplayName and the caption ProblemFolderLabel's;
       a pick cleared mid-quiz leaves the heading alone. *@
    <ScoreBreakdown Score="Controller.Score" Heading="Detailed evaluation">
        <HeadingLead>
            <ProblemFolderLabel Description="@Folder.DisplayName" />
        </HeadingLead>
    </ScoreBreakdown>

    <p class="text-muted">
        Total problems shown: <strong>@(ProblemsShown)</strong>.
    </p>

    @* Restart follows the one rule with no special case (SPEC-filtering.md §5,
       "Visible means in effect"; halheinrich/backgammon#5): it weights iff the
       mix panel is visible AT THIS MOMENT. So a run that was weighted can find
       the mix gone underneath it — the setting turned off from Settings
       mid-quiz, or a re-pick to a folder without stats — and the honest thing is
       to say so BEFORE the click rather than silently dropping the weighting or
       refusing after it. One sentence, and it names both ways the mix can have
       gone because this page cannot tell them apart: visibility is one fact,
       deliberately, and splitting it here to word this better would be a second
       copy of its halves. *@
    @if (RestartWillDropTheMix)
    {
        <p class="text-muted" role="status">
            This quiz was drawn from your weighted mix, but the mix isn't in
            effect now — it's switched off in <strong>Settings</strong>, or the
            folder in hand has no stats to weight by — so
            <strong>Restart</strong> runs unweighted.
        </p>
    }

    @* Weighted-restart refusal, mirroring Home's Start: a Restart that IS
       weighted composes against the stats document, and the file can have
       stopped being readable since the last run. That is now the only way here:
       a cleared pick or a folder without stats takes the panel off screen, which
       the rule above turns into an unweighted restart, not a refusal. The
       refusal touches no controller state — this summary stays intact — and the
       one-click per-run escape sits inside the notice; the stored mix is never
       touched. A gate reason, so not dismissible (SPEC-notices.md Fork C): it
       carries the escape button, and closing it would take that with it. *@
    @if (_mixRefused)
    {
        <Notice Kind="NoticeKind.Warning" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Assertive">
            <p class="mb-2">
                Lifetime stats aren't available for a restart
                (@MixDisplay.RefusalReason(StatsStore.Status)),
                so the weighted mix can't be applied.
            </p>
            <button class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm" id="restartWithoutMix"
                    disabled="@Controller.IsBusy"
                    @onclick="RestartWithoutMixAsync">
                Restart without mix
            </button>
            <span class="small text-muted ms-2">
                runs one quiz unweighted — or go Back to setup to change the
                mix; your saved mix is kept either way.
            </span>
        </Notice>
    }

    @* Restart disables while the controller is busy (the gate would no-op it
       anyway — this is the honest mirror); Back to setup stays enabled: it
       only navigates. *@
    <div class="d-flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" disabled="@Controller.IsBusy"
                @onclick="RestartAsync">
            Restart with same filters
        </button>
        <button class="btn btn-outline-secondary" @onclick="BackToSetup">
            Back to setup
        </button>
    </div>

    @* The third exit, stated because it is the one with no button (issue halheinrich/backgammon#51):
       whoever has just finished a quiz is exactly who wonders whether anything
       needs saving first. Nothing does — the lifetime record is written as each
       answer lands, not banked for the end.

       One line, and Help carries the reasons; this is an echo, not a second
       account. Gated on the two failure statuses the notices at the top of this
       page report, because "nothing needs saving" printed under "your stats
       could not be saved" reads as a contradiction — in those states the notice
       is the honest word and this would soften it. *@
    @if (StatsStore.Status is not (QuizStatsStatus.LoadFailed or QuizStatsStatus.WriteFailed))
    {
        <p class="text-muted small mt-3">
            Nothing here needs saving — you can close the tab whenever you like.
        </p>
    }
</div>
